WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.802
This site runs on a Raspberry Pi.

00:00:01.802 --> 00:00:06.388
I wanted its delivery machinery to live there too, alongside the rest of production.

00:00:06.388 --> 00:00:12.120
That constraint shaped the factory: small feedback loops, with the expensive verification done elsewhere.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.696
The ticket agent can read and edit files, but it has no shell.

00:00:16.696 --> 00:00:19.637
It cannot compile, run tests or download dependencies.

00:00:19.637 --> 00:00:26.280
GitHub Actions does that work, including the Docker based integration tests that would be a poor fit for this shared host.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.765
Signed GitHub webhooks reach one route through nginx.

00:00:30.765 --> 00:00:36.779
One software factory container holds the webhook receiver, Temporal workers, git and Claude Code.

00:00:36.779 --> 00:00:40.560
It integrates with GitHub, Linear, Loki and Dependency Track.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.760
Temporal keeps each workflow durable, with a task queue for each role.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:50.331
A separate deployer container holds the Docker socket and has no public ingress.

00:00:50.331 --> 00:00:55.360
Editing a repository and recreating production containers are separate responsibilities.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:57.866
This is the live console.

00:00:57.866 --> 00:01:02.099
The main loop runs from a ticket to a pull request, review, merge and deployment.

00:01:02.099 --> 00:01:04.764
Production logs feed new findings back into Linear.

00:01:04.764 --> 00:01:08.840
The vulnerability scan and the slower learning loop feed into the same system.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.331
The first walkthrough follows a pull request through review and verified deployment.

00:01:14.331 --> 00:01:17.498
Eligible changes can merge automatically under the path rules.

00:01:17.498 --> 00:01:19.541
Infrastructure changes require a person.

00:01:19.541 --> 00:01:24.240
The console exposes those boundaries rather than pretending every change is interchangeable.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:29.075
The second walkthrough starts with a small reported defect.

00:01:29.075 --> 00:01:33.387
I inspect a dry run, approve the ticket, and the agent proposes a fix.

00:01:33.387 --> 00:01:39.240
A red check becomes its next brief, with three attempts in total before it gives the work back.

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.853
The third follows a public vulnerability into a backend version update.

00:01:44.853 --> 00:01:47.028
GitHub verifies it, and I merge it.

00:01:47.028 --> 00:01:55.480
Dependency changes always carry no auto merge. npm fixes still come back to a person because the agent cannot regenerate their lockfile.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:00.943
That was my software factory: small delivery loops running on a Raspberry Pi.

00:02:00.943 --> 00:02:05.760
GitHub verifies the work, and I keep the decisions about approval and sensitive merges.
